82
00:08:05,616 --> 00:08:16,516
And what I'm going to do next is to trace where this idea actually came from, how it developed over the decades, and why it suddenly take off with large English models.

83
00:08:19,214 --> 00:08:28,612
If you look at the more linguistic side of agents, the word agent actually comes from Latin, meaning that one who acts.

84
00:08:29,072 --> 00:08:35,236
And this term also entered English in the late 14th century.

85
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:57,252
So in AI, the first index use of it is in this specific EGCAD paper from 1973, where Hewitt and Bishop and Steiger talk about this architecture, called actor algorithm, where each actor is an active agent that waits for a message and then does what the message tells them to do.

86
00:08:57,710 --> 00:09:04,868
So the idea of software entities that act on their own is older than most people can think about.

87
00:09:06,542 --> 00:09:18,020
And then I think like a few years after that, the contract net protocol actually talks about how agents dividing tasks with each other and the negotiating task.

88
00:09:19,278 --> 00:09:20,964
This trend actually continues.

89
00:09:21,390 --> 00:09:29,636
In Marvin Minsky's Society of Men's, it actually talk about how a group of sub-agents actually work together to get something done.

90
00:09:30,126 --> 00:09:36,420
And if you kind of reflect on this, this is actually the earlier idea of multi-agent orchestration.

91
00:09:38,414 --> 00:09:40,432
I think the history actually continues.

92
00:09:40,592 --> 00:09:48,996
Around the 1990s, we actually see more and more emergence of software systems that serve as agents.

93
00:09:49,712 --> 00:10:02,884
From PETI's designing autonomous agents to later people talk about agent as autonomy, social ability, reactivity, proactivity, trying to define agents in different ways.

94
00:10:03,758 --> 00:10:18,244
The most familiar definition to many of us is actually from Russell and Norvig, which defines agents as anything that perceives through sensors and acts through actuators here.

95
00:10:20,526 --> 00:10:26,116
We now kind of like enter a different stage, which we call reverse learning error here.

96
00:10:26,672 --> 00:10:33,316
This is a classic agent environmental loop that many of us get familiar during RL courses.

97
00:10:33,934 --> 00:10:41,508
So what happens here is that agents take action in the environment, and such action will receive a reward.

98
00:10:41,838 --> 00:10:45,924
And then they do the action based on specific state and the current reward.

99
00:10:46,350 --> 00:10:50,084
So this is a very classic agent environment loop from Saturn here.

100
00:10:50,990 --> 00:10:57,508
And then I think like we see more and more promising from promises from this kind of setup.

101
00:10:58,192 --> 00:11:02,192
DQN refers to deep Queue network.

102
00:11:02,592 --> 00:11:11,012
this actually from DeepMind earlier days, where they build AI systems to actually solve specific games.

103
00:11:12,110 --> 00:11:26,276
Now I think later you also see that AlphaGo and OpenAI5 uh showing that this kind of approach actually can reach superhuman performance in like Go and I think this one is DOTA 2.

104
00:11:26,926 --> 00:11:32,228
So those were agents, but each of them are actually trained for a specific task.

105
00:11:34,766 --> 00:11:38,244
So what actually changed is the introduction of large language models.

106
00:11:38,912 --> 00:11:44,804
I think the first kind of impressive capability of LMs is actually in-context learning.

107
00:11:45,902 --> 00:11:55,620
built upon this kind of transformer-based architectures, uh, GPT 3 actually showed that you can just talk about a task and give you a few demonstrations.

108
00:11:56,174 --> 00:11:58,610
Like in this particular example of Few shots.

109
00:11:59,182 --> 00:12:08,228
In order to do translation, you can offer certain examples, and then the model can actually follow such examples and then do the task.

110
00:12:08,750 --> 00:12:15,044
This entire process, unlike previous systems I showed you, there is no gradient update happening here.

111
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:28,000
I think like not only like in terms of prompting or in context learning, prompting could do more than teach a model a new task.

112
00:12:28,270 --> 00:12:36,580
It turned out that models themselves they can also reason step by step and then use tools for the things that they cannot do alone.

113
00:12:37,358 --> 00:12:40,720
So this particular example is called a chain of thought prompting.

114
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:49,348
You can see that asking the model to actually work through a problem step by step, actually give more accurate answers in general.

115
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:54,000
Web GPT was proposed by OpenAI.

116
00:12:54,734 --> 00:13:05,860
This actually enables language model in earlier days to do to use a web browser so that it can actually look up things online instead of only relying on memory or parameters.

117
00:13:07,150 --> 00:13:13,284
This actually lead to a lot, of follow-up work in terms of like web browser agent.

118
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:20,880
One of the very interesting architecture is actually called modular reasoning knowledge and language.

119
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:26,880
I would view it as one of the first earlier systems to think about to use.

120
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:34,080
So basically, this architecture actually decides which outside expert to send a request to.

121
00:13:34,382 --> 00:13:36,480
Is this going to be a weather request?

122
00:13:36,654 --> 00:13:38,820
So then I should use a weather API.

123
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:45,572
If this is a coding task, maybe I should send it to a Python programming tool, etc.

124
00:13:46,094 --> 00:13:51,332
So now the model actually can think and it can take actions through tools.

125
00:13:53,486 --> 00:13:56,560
So I think we now have like both important pieces here.

126
00:13:56,720 --> 00:14:01,476
That is, models can reason step by step and the models that can use tools.

127
00:14:01,934 --> 00:14:06,852
Those are two very, very important prerequisites for the emergence of agents.

128
00:14:07,470 --> 00:14:12,836
