407
00:43:35,712 --> 00:43:41,152
still, context window cannot hold all the event stream happen so far.

408
00:43:41,550 --> 00:43:51,552
So if you think about long horizon task we are talking about today, where agents can actually work on your project for more than three hours, that's actually a lot, a lot of data.

409
00:43:51,790 --> 00:43:54,724
And it's really hard to fit that into context window.

410
00:43:55,470 --> 00:44:05,232
The second is that even that's in uh that's possible, it may be really hard for the agent to figure out what is a relevant piece of information actually use.

411
00:44:05,742 --> 00:44:20,676
Think about that if you have 1000 documents there, you store them in your context, and then basically each time if you need to find the right uh um document, this is actually very kind of time consuming since you have such a big search space.

412
00:44:22,286 --> 00:44:34,148
so the bottom is actually a very interesting design from the generative agents, the Smallville project, where they were trying to build agents that can simulate people living in a small town.

413
00:44:34,606 --> 00:44:45,348
And then in their design, they actually need to use the memory stream to make sure that we can keep track of what happened to each individual so that the story can continue.

414
00:44:46,798 --> 00:44:49,732
There are different types of memories you can design.

415
00:44:50,512 --> 00:44:54,116
if you think about what kind of things to store.

416
00:44:54,672 --> 00:45:06,948
episodic memory here literally refers to we store everything, like raw logs, every sentence the agent has shared with users, every sentence that the users have provided to agents.

417
00:45:08,078 --> 00:45:19,748
Semantic memory means that instead of thinking about this kind of raw logs, we may want to remember like semantic events, since those may be more useful.

418
00:45:20,558 --> 00:45:23,072
Procedural memory, you store scales.

419
00:45:23,392 --> 00:45:31,812
Instead of like all the specificity, you remember like what is the scale, what is the procedural of how we do certain types of things.

420
00:45:32,462 --> 00:45:37,860
So if you go deep into this process, like this is actually an example of episodic memory.

421
00:45:38,112 --> 00:45:44,388
you can see that literally we are storing every like single time step, and then what happened at that time step.

422
00:45:46,222 --> 00:46:02,148
With semantic memory, we actually can do some synthesize of what happened over the agent trajectories, and then literally kind of like have this like a small summary box as a way to remember what has happened in the last several minutes.

423
00:46:02,638 --> 00:46:04,900
So this is what we mean by semantic memory.

424
00:46:06,254 --> 00:46:07,552
Um, procedural memory.

425
00:46:07,632 --> 00:46:14,052
you can actually check out this Voyager paper from Nvidia, where they were trying to teach agents to play.

426
00:46:14,512 --> 00:46:26,992
I think this is Minecraft here, where if the agent figure out how to use a new skill or a new tool, you actually want to abstract that into a skill and store it in your skill.

427
00:46:27,072 --> 00:46:27,552
library.

428
00:46:27,790 --> 00:46:33,392
And then whenever you have a new task, you want to see like, do I have a skill that I can use from a skill library?

429
00:46:33,632 --> 00:46:36,192
If so, then I'm going to retrieve it and use it.

430
00:46:36,526 --> 00:46:42,468
So this is more from a procedural perspective rather than like all the logs that have happened so far.

431
00:46:44,334 --> 00:46:45,604
So, this is about the memory.

